(* Go+ v0.14.0 grammar delta: proof arguments are mandatory.

   The complete base grammar is Go plus the declarations and expressions in
   grammar-v0.13.0.ebnf. No new syntax: this file records a change in what
   an existing form REQUIRES, and closes the unsoundness that
   grammar-v0.13.0.ebnf documented.
*)

(*
  An erased argument may be omitted and inferred — that is what makes
  `Length(t)` readable where the index `n` is recoverable from `t`. A
  PROOF argument may not.

  - A call to a function with a proposition parameter (`Eq`, `Le`, `Lt`)
    must supply that argument: `refl`/`decide` to have the decider prove
    it, or `assume` to assert it. Omitting it is an error naming the
    parameter, the proposition, and both remedies.
  - This is the fix for the unsoundness recorded in grammar-v0.13.0.ebnf.
    A proof argument is deleted by the same pass that discharges it, so a
    call that never had one is textually identical to one already erased,
    and the checker assumed the second. The obligation is now settled on
    the first resolve iteration — before any erasure exists — where the
    text is still exactly what the author wrote, so the two cases are
    distinguishable with certainty rather than by heuristic.
  - The check needs no type information, which is what lets it run that
    early: callees resolve through the file's imports and propositions are
    classified from marker text. On the first iteration a match arm's
    binders do not exist yet, so a type-directed check would give up in
    precisely the place recursive dependent code lives.
  - Ordinary erased indices are untouched. Inference remains for every
    parameter that does not carry a proposition.
  - **A proof-carrying function may only be used in a direct call.** It
    cannot be composed (`>>>`, `>=>`), piped, partially applied, or bound
    to a variable, because a proof can only be written at a call and each
    of those routes reaches generated Go without one. A piped stage is
    refused specifically: the piped value becomes the first argument,
    which is an erased parameter, so the form could never place the proof
    correctly. A dependent function with no proposition is unaffected.
  - REMAINING LIMIT, inherent rather than open: a plain Go caller of the
    generated artifact sees the erased signature and can call it with no
    proof. Obligations are enforced at the `.gp` boundary; generated Go
    rechecks exported inputs, not proofs.
*)
